 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Some embroidery techniques use mostly one colour:\n• White work: white thread on white fabric (cotton or linen); the beauty comes from the texture of the stitches, cut-outs and eyelets (small holes). Used for christening gowns, table linen, collars.\n• Candlewicking: knots (colonial or French knots) in thick, unbleached cotton thread on cream fabric, forming outlines and dotted designs. It took its name from the thread used to make candle wicks.\n• Shadow work: herringbone stitch worked on the back of a thin, see-through fabric (organza, voile), so the thread shows through softly as a 'shadow' on the front; outlines appear as back stitch.\nThese techniques need fine needles, careful tension and clean hands, because the fabric is light-coloured.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {},
  "problem": "Why must hands be very clean when doing white work?",
  "steps": [
   "White work uses white thread on white fabric.",
   "Any dirt or sweat would show and spoil the article."
  ],
  "answer": "Because dirt shows on white thread and fabric.",
  "checks": []
 },
